  "summary": "This page is about the music genre and subculture.",
  "description": "This page is about the music genre and subculture. For other aesthetics associated with the word \"industrial,\" see Industrial (disambiguation) .\n\nIndustrial is a genre of experimental music and the subculture that developed around it, whose members are sometimes called \"Industrialists,\" that originated in the mid-1970s, built on transgressive and provocative themes expressed through harsh, mechanical sound that its own artists termed \"anti-musical.\" The genre blended avant-garde electronic experiments, such as tape loops, musique concrète , and early synthesizers, with the confrontational ethos of punk , and its lyrical and thematic content favored controversial subjects like totalitarianism, violence, sexual perversion, and the occult, using shock value as a tool for social critique rather than simple provocation.\n\nThe term \"industrial music\" was coined by artist Monte Cazazza and adopted by the band Throbbing Gristle for their independent record label, Industrial Records, founded under the slogan \"industrial music for industrial people.\" By the 1980s the genre had moved past its experimental roots and split into a range of \"post-industrial\" styles, the most consequential of which was Electronic Body Music (EBM), which took industrial's harshness and paired it with danceable, sequenced rhythms. EBM built its own youth subculture around this sound, whose members were known as EBM-heads or, particularly in North America, Rivetheads , recognizable by a militaristic and fetish-inspired fashion that would go on to influence Cybergoth in the late 1990s.",
  "culturalContext": "Key values (per the Aesthetics Wiki): Provocation, confrontation, anti-conformity, transgression, social critique, experimentation, deprogramming",
